# modules/image/checks-rpm.rules - the container-image module's rpm-specific
# script-check registry (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9's `checks-<name>.rules` row,
# docs/FOUNDATION.md tension 29; data/scoursh-image-scan-design/report.md
# §4.1's check-set table and §5.3's IMG-12 row: "rpm - gated on the
# v1-distro-scope decision; requires-cmd: sqlite3").
#
# ONE FILE PER OWNING DRIVER, per report.md §5.1's binding rule (the
# cloud-wave `checks.rules` corruption, `c9f0e80`, is the incident that rule
# exists to prevent) and `modules/image/checks-apk.rules`'/
# `modules/image/checks-dpkg.rules`' own identical precedent: this ticket's
# OWN file, never an append to either of those or to a shared
# `modules/image/checks.rules`.
#
# `id: IMAGE-PKG-VULNERABLE_OS_PACKAGE-03` IS RPM-SPECIFIC, despite its
# generic-sounding name - the identical convention `checks-apk.rules`' `-01`
# and `checks-dpkg.rules`' `-02` already established.  A check id names one
# owning file under §9.1.1's uniqueness rule (E019), so apk's `-01` and
# dpkg's `-02` stay untouched and this ticket adds its own `-03` sibling in
# its own file, exactly as `checks-dpkg.rules`' own header predicted ("a
# future rpm ticket (IMG-12) adds a third sibling, -03, in its own
# checks-rpm.rules").
#
# `script: distro/rpm.sh` because a future rpm version comparator and the
# advisory-matching/finding-emission wiring a future Red Hat/Fedora
# advisory-ecosystem ticket adds are both expected to land in that same
# file, mirroring `checks-dpkg.rules`' own `script: distro/dpkg.sh` before
# a comparator/matcher landed on top of it.
#
# THIS CHECK DOES NOT FIRE YET (IMG-12's own scope: rpm enumeration only -
# no comparator, no advisories, no finding).  `distro/rpm.sh` ships an
# enumerator and no comparator, and nothing in `modules/image/run.sh` calls
# it - the three rpm database paths are extracted by
# `modules/image/acquire.sh`'s `IMAGE_METADATA_PATHS` (this ticket adds
# them) but are not yet parsed by anything `run.sh` reaches.  This is the
# identical "registered, not yet reachable" shape `checks-dpkg.rules` itself
# was in from IMG-07 through IMG-08/09.  `checks_registry_load` globs every
# `*.rules` file with no reachability requirement, so a check id registered
# here today costs nothing and is already correct the day a future rpm
# comparator/matcher ticket lands.
#
# `requires-cmd: sqlite3` - the modern rpm database backend (Fedora 33+,
# report.md §2.1) is a sqlite file, and this project ships no sqlite3
# implementation of its own; an rpm image scanned on a host without sqlite3
# on PATH must become a DECLARED coverage reduction
# (`rpm_db_binary_format`), never a silent clean pass.  This is the same
# precedent six DAST TLS checks already use for `openssl`
# (`modules/dast/passive/checks-tls.rules`).  It is registered here even
# though nothing calls this check yet, for the identical "correct the day
# it becomes reachable" reasoning the paragraph above gives - the check
# SELECTION chain (`lib/checks.sh`) already reads `requires-cmd` today, so a
# later ticket that wires this check into `run.sh` inherits the gate
# without having to remember to add it.
#
# `severity: high` is the REGISTERED DEFAULT, not a promise every finding
# will carry it - mirroring `checks-apk.rules`'/`checks-dpkg.rules`' own
# identical comment: the real severity is expected to come from
# `data/advisories.db`'s own per-advisory `severity` field once a matcher
# exists.
#
# `tags: passive` - rpm enumeration reads already-extracted local files and
# makes no network call and mutates nothing, the identical reasoning
# `checks-apk.rules`'/`checks-dpkg.rules`' own headers give for the same
# tag.  `tags: quick` for the identical reason those files give too.
#
# `coverage-scope: image-id` - the only legal value for the `IMAGE` module
# (rules/RULE-FORMAT.md §9.5.1's frozen table); the cell is the
# operator-declared stable id from `config/images.conf`, never the image
# digest or tag (report.md §3.4).

id: IMAGE-PKG-VULNERABLE_OS_PACKAGE-03
title: Installed rpm package matches a known advisory for this image's RHEL/Fedora release
script: distro/rpm.sh
severity: high
confidence: high
cwe: CWE-1104
owasp: A06:2021
tags: passive
tags: quick
coverage-scope: image-id
requires-cmd: sqlite3
remediation: Rebuild the image against an updated base layer (or upgrade
  the named package directly, where the Dockerfile installs it explicitly)
  so the installed version no longer matches this advisory, then re-scan.
  A base-image bump alone can carry this fix silently, so treat a `FROM`
  tag pin as something to review whenever this finding's advisory has a
  fixed-in version at or below the base image's own release.
references: data/scoursh-image-scan-design/report.md
references: CWE-1104
references: https://owasp.org/Top10/A06_2021-Vulnerable_and_Outdated_Components/
